.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="BETIsotherm generalizes Langmuir to multilayer adsorption. As the saturation vapor pressure P_0 \to \infty at fixed K=C/P_0, multilayer condensation is suppressed and BET reduces exactly to the Langmuir isotherm -- a reduction verified here numerically, not just asserted.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/surface/bet/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_bet_isotherm_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/surface/bet/plot_01_bet_isotherm`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">The BET multilayer adsorption isotherm</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/surface/bet/plot_01_bet_isotherm`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Polanyi (1914) described adsorption by the potential A = RT\ln(P_0/P) (~chemistrykit.surface.systems.dubinin.polanyi_potential) and claimed that the filled pore volume depends on A alone, through one characteristic curve that does not change with temperature. Dubinin and Radushkevich (1947) gave that curve for microporous carbons as W = W_0\exp[-(A/E)^2] (~chemistrykit.surface.systems.dubinin.dubinin_radushkevich_loading). Below, isotherms measured at three temperatures look different against P/P_0 but fall onto a single curve when replotted against A. A Dubinin-Radushkevich fit then recovers the pore volume W_0 and the characteristic energy E (~chemistrykit.surface.systems.dubinin.fit_dubinin_radushkevich).">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/surface/dubinin/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_polanyi_dubinin_characteristic_curve_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/surface/dubinin/plot_01_polanyi_dubinin_characteristic_curve`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Polanyi's potential theory and the Dubinin-Radushkevich characteristic curve</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/surface/dubinin/plot_01_polanyi_dubinin_characteristic_curve`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="FreundlichIsotherm models the empirical power law q=K_fP^{1/n}, useful for a heterogeneous surface where Langmuir&#x27;s single-site-energy assumption breaks down. Unlike Langmuir, it has no saturation limit, so extrapolating it far beyond the fitted pressure range is unphysical. fit_freundlich recovers (Kf, n) via the standard log q vs. log P linearization.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/surface/freundlich/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_freundlich_isotherm_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/surface/freundlich/plot_01_freundlich_isotherm`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">The Freundlich adsorption isotherm</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/surface/freundlich/plot_01_freundlich_isotherm`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="LangmuirIsotherm models monolayer adsorption on a fixed pool of identical surface sites. Its fractional coverage \theta=KP/(1+KP) reaches exactly one-half at the half-saturation pressure P=1/K, and fit_langmuir recovers (K, qmax) from noisy loading-vs-pressure data via the standard 1/q-vs-1/P linearization.">

